// View data — specs. A spec is a work package for an implementing agent:
// enough context, instructions, and acceptance criteria that the agent
// needs nothing else. These three are completed records kept as the bar for
// what a spec must contain. `wcl wad spec --from <rev>` writes new
// skeletons beside this one; add an import line here for each.
namespace wcl.wad

spec spec_node_table {
  title   = "node_table diagram shape"
  status  = :complete
  created = "2026-06-14"
  updated = "2026-06-20"
  owner   = "Wil Taylor"
  summary = "A titled row-table diagram shape with per-row connection ports, for DB/ER tables and UML class diagrams."
  affected = [wcl_wdoc]
  body {
    p "**Context.** ER and class diagrams need edges that land on a *row* (a column, a member), not on the whole box. No existing shape supports sub-shape ports."
    p "**Instructions.**"
    p "1. Add `node_table` / `node_row` blocks to the wdoc stdlib (`crates/wcl_wdoc/lib/node_table.wcl`) with stub `lower`s — the output (HTML rows measured inside an SVG frame) is not expressible in WCL, so the renderer special-cases them (per the Rust-special-cases ADR)."
    p "2. Implement the renderer in `crates/wcl_wdoc/src/node_table.rs`: the title bar and frame are SVG, the rows are HTML in a `foreignObject`; each row carrying an `id` registers as a sub-shape in the diagram's position index."
    p "3. Route edges: an edge whose endpoint names a row id must anchor on that row's left/right port instead of the table frame."
    p "**Acceptance.** A diagram with two `node_table`s and an edge from a row id to another row id renders with the edge anchored on both rows (integration test in `crates/wcl_wdoc/tests/`); the shape documents itself on the primitives page of the docs site; `just workspace-test` green."
  }
}

spec spec_diff_gitspec {
  title   = "Git revision operands for wcl diff"
  status  = :complete
  created = "2026-06-08"
  updated = "2026-06-12"
  owner   = "Wil Taylor"
  summary = "Either side of `wcl diff` may be `<rev>:<path>`; the revision's whole tree materialises so imports resolve."
  affected = [wcl_cli]
  body {
    p "**Context.** Diffing a working document against its last reviewed revision is the seam the WAD change workflow builds on — but a document's imports must resolve from the *same revision*, so checking out one file is not enough."
    p "**Instructions.**"
    p "1. Add a `<rev>:<path>` operand parser in `crates/wcl/src/gitspec.rs`: split on the first `:`, validate the revision with `git rev-parse`."
    p "2. Materialise the revision's whole tree into a temp dir via `git archive <rev> | tar -x`, then open `<path>` inside it — relative imports resolve against the materialised tree; the temp dir is removed when the diff completes."
    p "3. Wire both operands of `wcl diff` (`crates/wcl/src/diff.rs`) through the specifier: plain paths behave as before, either or both sides may be git specifiers."
    p "**Acceptance.** `wcl diff HEAD~1:doc.wcl doc.wcl` reports field-level changes with imports resolving from the old revision; a bad revision or path exits non-zero with a miette diagnostic naming it; integration tests cover both (`crates/wcl/tests/`)."
  }
}

spec spec_wad_format {
  title   = "WAD — the architecture document format"
  status  = :complete
  created = "2026-07-02"
  updated = "2026-07-02"
  owner   = "Wil Taylor"
  summary = "A typed architecture data model (this document), a twelve-chapter book projection, a `wcl init wad` scaffold, extractor scripts, and the LLM-decomposed spec workflow."
  affected = [wcl_cli, wcl_wdoc]
  body {
    p "**Context.** Architecture knowledge lived in prose and stale diagrams. The bet: model it as typed WCL data (arc42-inspired views over a C4 drill-down), derive every diagram from the data, and let agents work from the model instead of the rendered book."
    p "**Deliverables (all shipped, this document being the first WAD).**"
    p "1. The base schema as a canonical scaffold heredoc (`crates/wcl/src/scaffold/templates/wad.wcl`, terminator `WAD_SCHEMA_BASE_WCL`) with drift gates (`just wad-schema-sync` / `wad-schema-check`, CI-enforced)."
    p "2. The book projection under `wdoc/book/` — pure templates, no content: node index, relation roll-up, one page file per view."
    p "3. The `wcl init wad` scaffold and the `wcl wad spec --from <rev>` seeder (`crates/wcl/src/wad.rs`) that pins the baseline sha and the mechanical change list of a new spec."
    p "4. Extractor scripts under `.wad/scripts/` (uv single-file Python) owning `data/generated/` — cargo metadata, CI workflows, the serve HTTP API."
    p "5. The `wad` wskill teaching the format, plus the classification rules (personas are users; hosting platforms are not externals; documentation is an output, not a system) and ADR-008/ADR-009."
    p "**Acceptance (met).** `wcl check .wad/wad.wcl` green; the book renders all chapters with derived diagrams; `wcl init wad` scaffolds a WAD that checks and builds; a data edit round-trips into a spec skeleton via `wcl wad spec --from <rev>`."
  }
}
